half-plane\n- discrete-time stability: spectrum strictly inside the unit disk\
\n\nFor symbolic finite-dimensional surrogates, we use eigenvalues directly.\n\
For PDE or operator models, we use mode truncations and then inspect \
convergence of the stability verdict as truncation increases.\n\n### 2.1 \
Finite-dimensional spectral diagnostics\n\n```wl\nClearAll[spectralData];\n\n\
spectralData[matrix_?MatrixQ] := Module[\n  {vals},\n  vals = \
Eigenvalues[matrix];\n  <|\n    \"Eigenvalues\" -> vals,\n    \"RealParts\" \
-> Re[vals],\n    \"ImaginaryParts\" -> Im[vals],\n    \
\"ContinuousTimeStableQ\" -> And @@ Thread[Re[vals] < 0],\n    \
\"DiscreteTimeStableQ\" -> And @@ Thread[Abs[vals] < 1],\n    \
\"StabilityMarginContinuous\" -> -Max[Re[vals]],\n    \
\"StabilityMarginDiscrete\" -> 1 - Max[Abs[vals]]\n  |>\n];\n```\n\n### 2.2 \
Mode-truncation wrapper for infinite-dimensional approximations\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[truncationStabilityTable];\n\n\
truncationStabilityTable[operatorBuilder_, modeCounts_List] :=\n  \
AssociationMap[\n    spectralData[operatorBuilder[#]] &,\n    modeCounts\n  \
];\n```\n\n### 2.3 Example: damped nearest-neighbor operator family\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[dampedLaplacian1D];\n\ndampedLaplacian1D[n_Integer?Positive, \
\[Gamma]_?NumericQ] := Module[\n  {lap},\n  lap = SparseArray[\n    {\n      \
Band[{1, 1}] -> -2,\n      Band[{1, 2}] -> 1,\n      Band[{2, 1}] -> 1\n    \
},\n    {n, n}\n  ] // Normal;\n  lap - \[Gamma] IdentityMatrix[n]\n];\n\n\
exampleSpectral = truncationStabilityTable[\n  dampedLaplacian1D[#, 0.3] &,\n \
 {8, 16, 32, 64}\n];\n\nexampleSpectral\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Scale invariance \
as coarse-graining fixed-point testing\n\nA system is scale-invariant if an \
appropriate coarse-graining transformation leaves the effective description \
unchanged up to rescaling.\n\nWe encode this in a practical way:\n\n- define \
a coarse-graining transform\n- iterate it\n- test whether the object \
approaches a fixed point\n- measure the residual under renormalization\n\n### \
3.1 Generic fixed-point residual\n\n```wl\nClearAll[fixedPointResidual];\n\n\
fixedPointResidual[transform_, state_, norm_: Norm] :=\n  \
norm[Flatten[{transform[state] - state}]];\n```\n\n### 3.2 Iterated RG-style \
evolution\n\n```wl\nClearAll[rgOrbit];\n\nrgOrbit[transform_, state_, \
steps_Integer?NonNegative] :=\n  NestList[transform, state, steps];\n```\n\n\
### 3.3 Fixed-point test\n\n```wl\nClearAll[scaleFixedPointData];\n\n\
scaleFixedPointData[transform_, state_, steps_Integer : 12, tol_ : 10^-10] := \
Module[\n  {orbit, residuals, finalResidual},\n  orbit = rgOrbit[transform, \
state, steps];\n  residuals = fixedPointResidual[transform, #] & /@ orbit;\n  \
finalResidual = Last[residuals];\n  <|\n    \"Orbit\" -> orbit,\n    \
\"Residuals\" -> residuals,\n    \"FinalResidual\" -> finalResidual,\n    \
\"FixedPointQ\" -> finalResidual < tol\n  |>\n];\n```\n\n### 3.4 Example: \
dyadic coarse-graining of a signal\n\nThe simplest scale test uses local \
averaging followed by normalization.\n\n```wl\nClearAll[dyadicCoarseGrain];\n\
\ndyadicCoarseGrain[list_List] /; EvenQ[Length[list]] :=\n  Normalize[Mean /@ \
Partition[list, 2], Norm];\n\nexampleSignal = Normalize[Table[1/Sqrt[i], {i, \
1, 64}], Norm];\nexampleScale = scaleFixedPointData[dyadicCoarseGrain, \
exampleSignal, 5, 10^-6];\n\nexampleScale[\"FinalResidual\"]\n```\n\n---\n\n\
## 4. Incommensurable self-measurement and pointer-state selection\n\nThe \
correct rigorous implementation is not mystical. We model:\n\n- a system \
basis\n- a decohering or dephasing channel\n- incommensurable or \
quasiperiodic phase forcing\n- survival of robust modes / basis states\n\nThe \
key object is the long-time persistence of components under repeated \
evolution plus measurement/noise.\n\n### 4.1 Simple dephasing-plus-phase \
channel\n\n```wl\nClearAll[dephasingChannel];\n\n\
dephasingChannel[state_?MatrixQ, p_?NumericQ] := Module[\n  {diag, offDiag},\n\
  diag = DiagonalMatrix[Diagonal[state]];\n  offDiag = state - diag;\n  diag \
+ (1 - p) offDiag\n];\n```\n\n### 4.2 Quasiperiodic phase evolution\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[phaseUnitary];\n\nphaseUnitary[phases_List] := DiagonalMatrix[Exp[I \
phases]];\n```\n\n### 4.3 One-step self-measurement evolution\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[selfMeasurementStep];\n\nselfMeasurementStep[state_?MatrixQ, \
phases_List, p_?NumericQ] := Module[\n  {u},\n  u = phaseUnitary[phases];\n  \
dephasingChannel[u . state . ConjugateTranspose[u], p]\n];\n```\n\n### 4.4 \
Iterate and identify pointer basis weights\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[pointerModeData];\n\npointerModeData[initialState_?MatrixQ, \
phaseSchedule_List, p_?NumericQ] := Module[\n  {orbit, finalState, weights},\n\
  orbit = FoldList[selfMeasurementStep[#1, #2, p] &, initialState, \
phaseSchedule];\n  finalState = Last[orbit];\n  weights = \
Chop[Diagonal[finalState]];\n  <|\n    \"Orbit\" -> orbit,\n    \
\"FinalState\" -> finalState,\n    \"PointerWeights\" -> weights,\n    \
\"DominantPointerIndex\" -> First @ Ordering[weights, -1]\n  |>\n];\n```\n\n\
### 4.5 Example: quasiperiodic phase schedule\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[quasiPeriodicSchedule];\n\n\
quasiPeriodicSchedule[steps_Integer?Positive, dim_Integer?Positive] :=\n  \
Table[\n    Table[n Sqrt[j], {j, 1, dim}],\n    {n, 1, steps}\n  ];\n\n\
initialState2 = {{1, 1}, {1, 1}}/2;\nexamplePointer = pointerModeData[\n  \
initialState2,\n  quasiPeriodicSchedule[20, 2],\n  0.15\n];\n\n\
examplePointer[\"FinalState\"] // MatrixForm\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Unified \
multiscale stability report\n\nNow we combine the three diagnostics. This \
does **not** assume they are one law. It simply computes the three criteria \
side by side and reports their intersection.\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[multiscaleStabilityReport];\n\nmultiscaleStabilityReport[\n  \
matrix_?MatrixQ,\n  transform_,\n  state_,\n  initialDensity_?MatrixQ,\n  \
phaseSchedule_List,\n  p_?NumericQ,\n  steps_Integer : 12,\n  tol_ : 10^-8\n] \
:= Module[\n  {\n    spec,\n    scale,\n    pointer,\n    verdict\n  },\n  \
spec = spectralData[matrix];\n  scale = scaleFixedPointData[transform, state, \
steps, tol];\n  pointer = pointerModeData[initialDensity, phaseSchedule, p];\n\
  verdict = <|\n    \"SpectrallyStableQ\" -> \
TrueQ[spec[\"ContinuousTimeStableQ\"]],\n    \"ScaleInvariantQ\" -> \
TrueQ[scale[\"FixedPointQ\"]],\n    \"PointerStateSelectedQ\" -> Max[pointer[\
\"PointerWeights\"]] > 1/2\n  |>;\n  <|\n    \"Spectral\" -> spec,\n    \
\"Scale\" -> scale,\n    \"Pointer\" -> pointer,\n    \"Verdict\" -> verdict,\
\n    \"AllThreeQ\" -> And @@ Values[verdict]\n  |>\n];\n```\n\n### 5.1 \
Example unified run\n\n```wl\nexampleMatrix = dampedLaplacian1D[8, 0.5];\n\
exampleState = Normalize[Table[1/Sqrt[i], {i, 1, 64}], Norm];\nexampleDensity \
= {{1, 1}, {1, 1}}/2;\nexamplePhases = quasiPeriodicSchedule[20, 2];\n\n\
exampleUnified = multiscaleStabilityReport[\n  exampleMatrix,\n  \
dyadicCoarseGrain,\n  exampleState,\n  exampleDensity,\n  examplePhases,\n  \
0.15,\n  5,\n  10^-6\n];\n\nexampleUnified[\"Verdict\"]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. \
Symbolic operator route for field models\n\nFor OPF-style field models, the \
right next move is to linearize the Euler\[Dash]Lagrange equations around a \
stationary solution and extract the linearized operator.\n\nThe stability \
question then becomes:\n\n- what is the spectrum of the linearized operator?\n\
- does coarse-graining preserve the effective form?\n- which linear \
combinations survive under repeated decohering interaction?\n\n### 6.1 \
Template for linearization around an equilibrium\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[linearizeFieldEquations];\n\nlinearizeFieldEquations[eqns_, \
fields_List, perturbations_List, \[CurlyEpsilon]sym_: \[CurlyEpsilon]] := \
Module[\n  {rules, expanded},\n  rules = Thread[fields -> (fields + \
\[CurlyEpsilon]sym perturbations)];\n  expanded = Normal @ Series[eqns /. \
rules, {\[CurlyEpsilon]sym, 0, 1}];\n  Coefficient[expanded, \
\[CurlyEpsilon]sym, 1] == 0\n];\n```\n\nYou can apply this to the OPF \
extended Euler equations once a nontrivial equilibrium and, ideally, a \
time-dependent kinetic structure are specified.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Mode \
extraction for PDE models\n\nIf the linearized field equations are PDEs over \
a region, numerical eigenmode analysis is the practical route.\n\nRelevant \
built-in functions are:\n\n- [NDEigenvalues](paclet:ref/NDEigenvalues)\n- \
[NDEigensystem](paclet:ref/NDEigensystem)\n- \
[DiscretizedPDEData](paclet:FEMDocumentation/ref/DiscretizedPDEData)\n\nThe \
practical workflow is:\n\n1. derive or specify the linearized PDE operator\n\
2. impose admissible boundary conditions\n3. compute eigenvalues / \
eigenfunctions\n4. identify stable, unstable, and marginal modes\n\n### 7.1 \
PDE eigenmode template\n\n```wl\n(*** template only; requires a specific PDE \
and region ***)\n(*** evals = NDEigenvalues[{op[u[x, y]] == \[Lambda] u[x, \
y], bc}, u[x, y], {x, y} \[Element] region, n]; ***)\n```\n\nFor operator \
stability, remember:\n\n- in continuous-time settings, stable decay \
corresponds to spectra in the left half-plane\n- in second-order oscillatory \
problems, mode frequencies often come from square roots of eigenvalues\n- \
unconstrained systems can exhibit rigid-body or zero modes that must be \
interpreted separately\n\n---\n\n## 8. What counts as evidence for the \
synthesis hypothesis?\n\nThe synthesis hypothesis would gain support if, \
across diverse systems, one repeatedly found that:\n\n1. spectral stability \
and scale fixed-point behavior coincide,\n2. the same modes are selected by \
decohering quasiperiodic forcing,\n3. the three diagnostics converge under \
refinement rather than drifting apart.\n\nThis can be tested empirically and \
computationally.\n\n```wl\nClearAll[synthesisEvidenceScore];\n\n\
synthesisEvidenceScore[report_Association] := Mean @ Boole @ \
Values[report[\"Verdict\"]];\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9. Interactive exploration\n\n\
```wl\nManipulate[\n Module[\n  {\n   mat,\n   state,\n   density,\n   \
phases,\n   report\n  },\n  mat = dampedLaplacian1D[n, \[Gamma]];\n  state = \
Normalize[Table[1/i^\[Alpha], {i, 1, 2^m}], Norm];\n  density = {{1, c}, \
{Conjugate[c], 1}}/2;\n  phases = quasiPeriodicSchedule[steps, 2];\n  report \
= multiscaleStabilityReport[\n    mat,\n    dyadicCoarseGrain,\n    state,\n  \
  density,\n    phases,\n    p,\n    Min[m - 1, 8],\n    tol\n  ];\n  \
Column[{\n    Style[\"Unified Multiscale Stability Report\", 14, Bold],\n    \
report[\"Verdict\"],\n    Row[{\"All three criteria met: \", \
report[\"AllThreeQ\"]}],\n    Row[{\"Continuous-time stability margin: \", \
report[\"Spectral\", \"StabilityMarginContinuous\"]}],\n    Row[{\"Scale \
fixed-point residual: \", report[\"Scale\", \"FinalResidual\"]}],\n    \
Row[{\"Pointer weights: \", report[\"Pointer\", \"PointerWeights\"]}]\n  }]\n \
],\n {{n, 8, \"matrix size\"}, 4, 24, 2},\n {{\[Gamma], 0.5, \"damping\"}, \
0.05, 2, 0.05},\n {{\[Alpha], 0.5, \"power-law exponent\"}, 0.1, 2, 0.05},\n \
{{m, 6, \"signal exponent length 2^m\"}, 3, 9, 1},\n {{c, 0.8, \"initial \
coherence\"}, 0, 1},\n {{p, 0.15, \"dephasing strength\"}, 0, 1, 0.01},\n \
{{steps, 20, \"phase steps\"}, 5, 60, 1},\n {{tol, 10^-6, \"fixed-point \
tolerance\"}, 10^-10, 10^-3, ControlType -> SetterBar}\n]\n```\n\n---\n\n## \
10. Next rigorous extension for OPF specifically\n\nTo connect this notebook \
back to the OPF derivation, the next mathematically meaningful steps are:\n\n\
1. add a true time-dependent kinetic structure to the OPF action\n2. derive \
the linearized field operator around a nontrivial equilibrium\n3. compute its \
modal spectrum\n4. define an explicit coarse-graining transform on the field \
variables or couplings\n5. define a measurement/decoherence channel on the \
mode amplitudes\n6. test whether the same modes are:\n   - spectrally stable,\
\n   - RG-persistent,\n   - pointer-selected\n\nOnly then can the proposed \
synthesis be evaluated as more than an analogy.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Summary\n\n\
This notebook does **not** assume the universal claim is already proven.\n\n\
It instead provides a test harness for the precise question:\n\n> when do \
spectral stability, scale invariance, and incommensurable measurement \
selection coincide in one and the same system?\n\nThat is the correct \
computational path.",
